ARTICLE 9: FORCE MAJEURE

9.1 Neither Party shall be liable to the other for any failure or delay in performing its obligations under this Agreement to the extent that such failure or delay is caused by a Force Majeure Event. For the purposes of this Agreement, a "Force Majeure Event" means any event or circumstance beyond the reasonable control of the affected Party, including but not limited to:

(a) acts of God, flood, drought, earthquake, storm, or other natural disaster;

(b) epidemic or pandemic;

(c) acts of war (whether declared or undeclared), hostilities, invasion, act of foreign enemy, blockade, or embargo;

(d) terrorism or threat of terrorism;

(e) riot, civil commotion, strike, lockout, or other industrial action (other than strikes or lockouts involving the affected Party's own employees where the affected Party could reasonably have avoided or mitigated the same by accepting the terms of a collective bargaining agreement);

(f) fire, explosion, or accident;

(g) shortage or unavailability of fuel, power, or water;

